A module of console output, decimal formatting, and byte-safe input.

Use core.

To format n (an unsigned number) into buf (a pointer to byte), named format_u64_into, giving an unsigned number.
Let digits be block of 20 byte.
Let count be 0 as an unsigned number.
Let value be n.
If value equals 0, set item 0 of buf to 48, give back 1.
While value is more than 0, set item count of digits to 48 plus (value modulo 10) as a byte, set value to value divided by 10, add 1 to count.
Let len be 0 as an unsigned number.
While count is more than 0, set count to count minus 1, set item len of buf to item count of digits, add 1 to len.
Give back len.

Publicly, to format n (a whole number) into buf (a pointer to byte), named format_i64_into, giving an unsigned number.
Let len be 0 as an unsigned number.
Let magnitude be 0 as an unsigned number.
If n is less than 0, set item 0 of buf to 45, set len to 1 as an unsigned number, set magnitude to (0 as an unsigned number) minus (n as an unsigned number), otherwise set magnitude to n as an unsigned number.
Let written be format magnitude into (buf as an unsigned number plus len) as a pointer to byte.
Give back len plus written.

Publicly, to format x (a decimal) into buf (a pointer to byte), named format_f64_into, giving an unsigned number.
Let negative be false.
Let absolute be x.
If x is less than 0.0, set negative to true, set absolute to 0.0 minus x.
Let scaled be (absolute times 10000.0 plus 0.5) as an unsigned number.
Let integer part be scaled divided by 10000.
Let frac be scaled modulo 10000.
Let len be 0 as an unsigned number.
If negative and scaled is not equal to 0, set item 0 of buf to 45, set len to 1 as an unsigned number.
Let text length be format integer part into (buf as an unsigned number plus len) as a pointer to byte.
Set len to len plus text length.
Set item len of buf to 46.
Set len to len plus 1.
Let divisor be 1000 as an unsigned number.
While divisor is more than 0, set item len of buf to (48 plus frac divided by divisor) as a byte, set frac to frac modulo divisor, set divisor to divisor divided by 10, add 1 to len.
Give back len.

To print a newline, named newline.
Ask Linux kernel 1 with 1, "\n", and 1.

Publicly, to write p (a pointer to byte) of length n (an unsigned number), named write_bytes.
Ask Linux kernel 1 with 1, p, and n.

Publicly, to write a whole number n, named write_i64.
Let buf be block of 32 byte.
Let len be format n into address of item 0 of buf.
Ask Linux kernel 1 with 1, address of item 0 of buf, and len.

Publicly, to write an unsigned number n, named write_u64.
Let buf be block of 32 byte.
Let len be format n into address of item 0 of buf.
Ask Linux kernel 1 with 1, address of item 0 of buf, and len.

Publicly, to write a decimal x, named write_f64.
Let buf be block of 64 byte.
Let len be format x into address of item 0 of buf.
Ask Linux kernel 1 with 1, address of item 0 of buf, and len.

Publicly, to write a truth value b, named write_bool.
If b, ask Linux kernel 1 with 1, "true", and 4, otherwise ask Linux kernel 1 with 1, "false", and 5.

Publicly, to end the line, named write_newline.
Ask Linux kernel 1 with 1, "\n", and 1.

Publicly, to say n (a whole number), named print_i64.
Let buf be block of 32 byte.
Let len be format n into address of item 0 of buf.
Ask Linux kernel 1 with 1, address of item 0 of buf, and len.
Print a newline.

Publicly, to say n (an unsigned number), named print_u64.
Let buf be block of 32 byte.
Let len be format n into address of item 0 of buf.
Ask Linux kernel 1 with 1, address of item 0 of buf, and len.
Print a newline.

Publicly, to say x (a decimal), named print_f64.
Let buf be block of 64 byte.
Let len be format x into address of item 0 of buf.
Ask Linux kernel 1 with 1, address of item 0 of buf, and len.
Print a newline.

Publicly, to say b (a truth value), named print_bool.
If b, ask Linux kernel 1 with 1, "true", and 4, otherwise ask Linux kernel 1 with 1, "false", and 5.
Print a newline.

Publicly, to show n (a whole number), named show_i64.
Say n.

Publicly, to show n (an unsigned number), named show_u64.
Say n.

Publicly, to show x (a decimal), named show_f64.
Say x.

Publicly, to show b (a truth value), named show_bool.
Say b.

Publicly, to print n (a whole number), named print_synonym_i64.
Say n.

Publicly, to print n (an unsigned number), named print_synonym_u64.
Say n.

Publicly, to print x (a decimal), named print_synonym_f64.
Say x.

Publicly, to print b (a truth value), named print_synonym_bool.
Say b.

Publicly, to display n (a whole number), named display_i64.
Say n.

Publicly, to display n (an unsigned number), named display_u64.
Say n.

Publicly, to display x (a decimal), named display_f64.
Say x.

Publicly, to display b (a truth value), named display_bool.
Say b.

Publicly, to say bytes p (a pointer to byte) of length len (an unsigned number), named print_bytes.
Ask Linux kernel 1 with 1, p, and len.
Print a newline.

Publicly, to read a whole number from input, named read_i64_stdin, giving a whole number.
Let ch be block of 1 byte.
Let value be 0.
Let sign be 1.
Let seen be false.
Let done be false.
Let read count be 1.
Let code be 0.
While done is false.
Set read count to ask Linux kernel 0 with 0, address of item 0 of ch, and 1.
If read count is at most 0, set done to true.
If done is false, set code to (item 0 of ch) as a whole number.
If done is false and seen is false and code equals 45, set sign to -1.
If done is false and seen is false and code equals 32, set done to false.
If done is false and seen is false and code equals 9, set done to false.
If done is false and seen is false and code equals 13, set done to false.
If done is false and seen is false and code equals 10, set done to false.
If done is false and code is at least 48 and code is at most 57, set value to value times 10 plus (code minus 48), set seen to true.
If done is false and seen is true and (code is less than 48 or code is more than 57), set done to true.
End the loop.
Give back value times sign.

Publicly, to read a decimal from input, named read_f64_stdin, giving a decimal.
Let ch be block of 1 byte.
Let value be 0.0.
Let sign be 1.0.
Let divisor be 10.0.
Let seen be false.
Let fractional be false.
Let done be false.
Let read count be 1.
Let code be 0.
While done is false.
Set read count to ask Linux kernel 0 with 0, address of item 0 of ch, and 1.
If read count is at most 0, set done to true.
If done is false, set code to (item 0 of ch) as a whole number.
If done is false and seen is false and fractional is false and code equals 45, set sign to -1.0.
If done is false and seen is false and fractional is false and code equals 32, set done to false.
If done is false and seen is false and fractional is false and code equals 9, set done to false.
If done is false and seen is false and fractional is false and code equals 13, set done to false.
If done is false and seen is false and fractional is false and code equals 10, set done to false.
If done is false and fractional is false and code equals 46, set seen to true, set fractional to true.
If done is false and fractional and code is at least 48 and code is at most 57, set value to value plus (code minus 48) as a decimal divided by divisor, set divisor to divisor times 10.0.
If done is false and fractional is false and code is at least 48 and code is at most 57, set value to value times 10.0 plus (code minus 48) as a decimal, set seen to true.
If done is false and fractional and code is not 46 and (code is less than 48 or code is more than 57), set done to true.
If done is false and fractional is false and seen is true and code is not 46 and (code is less than 48 or code is more than 57), set done to true.
End the loop.
Give back value times sign.

Publicly, to read number into dst (a pointer to whole number), named read_i64_into.
Call this as "read number into <dst>".
Call this as "ask for <dst>".
Let number read be read a whole number from input.
Set item 0 of dst to number read.
